<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pehr Evind Svinhufvud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pehr Evind Svinhufvud was a Finnish statesman who led the Senate that declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>'s independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on 6 December 1917. He was regent during the attempt to make Finland a monarchy in 1918 and later served as prime minister and as president from 1931 to 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pehr-evind-svinhufvud.jpg" alt="Pehr Evind Svinhufvud" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pehr_Evind_Svinhufvud_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pehr Evind Svinhufvud af Qvalstad was born in Sääksmäki into a Finland-Swedish noble family that had been ennobled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1574 and introduced to the Finnish House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobility">Nobility</a> in 1818. His father, a sea captain, drowned off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1863. After his grandfather's death in 1866 he moved to Helsinki with his mother and sister, studied at the Imperial Alexander University and took a Master of Laws degree in 1886. He worked as a lawyer and judge and sat in the Estate of Nobles in the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> began its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> policy in 1899, Svinhufvud became a central figure in the legal resistance. In 1902 the Turku Court of Appeal began proceedings against Governor-General Bobrikov, who answered by dismissing sixteen of its officials, Svinhufvud among them. He was elected to the new Parliament in 1906 and was its Speaker from 1907 to 1912; his opening speeches on legality led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> to dissolve Parliament in 1909 and 1910. In November 1914 he was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomsk">Tomsk</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> after refusing the orders of a Russian procurator, and he returned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a>.</p><p>He became Chairman of the Senate on 27 November 1917. With Carl Enckell and Gustaf Idman he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, where the Soviet government agreed to recognise Finnish independence at the end of December. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Finnish Civil War</a> he led the White government while Mannerheim commanded its armies, and he pardoned 36,000 Red prisoners in the autumn of 1918. Named Regent in May 1918, he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> to ask <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> to allow Prince Oskar to become king; the monarchy was for him a means of securing German support. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat he stepped down in favour of Mannerheim.</p><p>After the war he withdrew from public life and took up the Civil Guard and competitive shooting. Prime minister from 1930 on the insistence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapua-movement">Lapua Movement</a>, he won the presidential election of 1931 by 151 votes to 149 against Ståhlberg. In February 1932 he ended the Mäntsälä Rebellion with a radio speech calling on the rebels to surrender. He left most foreign policy to his foreign ministers, and in 1937 the Social Democrats and the Agrarian party voted against him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Svinhufvud died at Luumäki in 1944. He is praised for his commitment to legality under Russian rule and for his firm handling of the Mäntsälä Rebellion, and criticised for one-sided political activity and for relying wholly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1918. Known to many as Ukko-Pekka, or Old Man Pekka, he was widely popular. A statue by the sculptor Wäinö Aaltonen was erected outside the Parliament House in 1961.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pehr Evind Svinhufvud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Russian Revolution of 1917 the Finns moved towards separation, and on 6 December 1917 parliament approved the declaration of independence presented by the Senate under <b>Pehr Evind Svinhufvud</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pehr Evind Svinhufvud</b> served as regent.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-finland-1918">Kingdom of Finland (1918)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Kotkaniemi, in what is now <a href="/countries/finland">Finland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=60.97,27.83&amp;year=1914" aria-label="Kotkaniemi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Pehr Evind Svinhufvud"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4910 4916h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4910 4916h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=60.97,27.83&amp;year=1914">What was here in 1914? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
